1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clara_Jessup_Moore
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara Sophia Jessup Bloomfield-Moore (February 16, 1824 – January 5, 1899)[1] was an American philanthropist and philosopher.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Westfield, Massachusetts. She married businessman Bloomfield Haines Moore (1819-1878) and resided in Philadelphia from the date of her marriage onward. Following the death of her husband she moved to London, where 
2 CONC she eventually died in 1899. She organized in Philadelphia a hospital relief committee during the American Civil War and assisted in the foundation of the Temperance Home for Children.
2 CONT
2 CONT She and her husband had three children: Ella Carlton Moore (1843–1892), Clarence Bloomfield Moore (1852–1936), Lilian Stuart Moore (1853–1911).
2 CONT
2 CONT Philosophy
2 CONT
2 CONT Her books on etiquette connected the perennial philosophy to social behavior; for instance, she described harmony as the basis of good manners: "the secret or essence of good manners, as of goodness in all other things, consists i
2 CONC n suitableness, or in other words of harmony." She promoted a "science of social intercourse" consisting of "the means through which people meet each other, maintaining harmony and peace in their relations, and securing the greate
2 CONC st possible amount of pleasure and comfort to all." This philosophy was subsequently applied to physics. Her book on ether was written because she believed that ether could account for the operation of the motor invented by John E
2 CONC rnst Worrell Keely, to whose Keely Motor Company she gave liberally in order that he might develop his idea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Selected works
2 CONT
2 CONT Miscellaneous Poems (1875)
2 CONT
2 CONT On Dangerous Ground (1876), a romance
2 CONT
2 CONT Sensible Etiquette (1878)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ether the True Protoplasm (1885)
2 CONT
2 CONT Social Ethics and Social Duties (1892)
