1 OCCU Archaeologist, expert in Native American archaelogy
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was born in Philadelphia January 14, 1852 and died in St. Petersburg, FL, on March 24, 1936, unmarried. He received his B.A. from Harvard University in 1873 and, in 1876. He traveled across South America, over t
2 CONC he Andes and down the Amazon. Clarence B. Moore was an expert in Native American archaelogy  and is remembered today for his excavations of North American Indian mounds and cemeteries in the southern United States. He was a member
2 CONC  of the Photographic Society of Philadelphia.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clarence_Bloomfield_Moore
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarence Bloomfield Moore (January 14, 1852 – March 24, 1936), more commonly known as C.B. Moore, was an American archaeologist and writer. He studied and excavated Native American sites in the Southeastern United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of writer Clara Jessup Moore, and businessman Bloomfield Haines Moore (1819–1878), he earned a Bachelor of Arts degree at Harvard University in 1873. He traveled in nearly every part of Europe, Asia Minor, and Egypt; he cr
2 CONC ossed the Andes and went down the Amazon River in 1876, and made a trip around the world in 1878–79, before returning home when his father died in 1878.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death, Moore became the president of the family company, Jessup & Moore Paper Company, retained that role for the majority of the 1880s, and earned millions during his tenure. By the late 1880s, he was eager to 
2 CONC pursue his lifelong interest in archaeology and turned over company management to others.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1892 to 1894, Moore performed excavations at St. Johns Shell Middens in Florida. Between 1897 and 1898, he also dug at the Irene Mound (outside Savannah, Georgia) and exhumed seven human skeletons. He accessed many of these s
2 CONC ites by water, in his steamboat named the Gopher. Over a period of 20 years, he explored Indian mounds in nearly all the Southern states. His writings, for the most part published by the Academy of Natural Sciences in Philadelphia
2 CONC , include "Some Aboriginal Sites in Louisiana and in Arkansas" (1913).
2 CONT
2 CONT Moore was elected a member of the American Antiquarian Society in 1895.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1990, in his honor, the Lower Mississippi Valley Survey of Harvard University, in conjunction with the Southeastern Archaeological Conference, created the C.B. Moore Award for Excellence in Southeastern Archaeology by a Young S
2 CONC cholar.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Clarence B. Moore House was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1973.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clarence_Bloomfield_Moore#Works
