1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amelie_Posse
2 CONT
2 CONT Amelie Posse-Brázdová (11 February 1884 in Stockholm – 3 March 1957) was a Swedish author. She is also known for her work against nazism during World War II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amelie Posse was the daughter of Count Fredrik Arvidsson Posse and Auda Gunhild Wennerberg. She was married to the criminal psychologist Andreas Bjerre in 1904–1912 and to the Czech artist Oskar (nicknamed Oki) Brázda (1887–1977) 
2 CONC from 1915, and became the mother of Bohuslav (Slavo) 9/13/1916-1991;RAF pilot) & the artist Jan Brazda(12/4/1917-2012).
2 CONT
2 CONT During her second marriage, she lived in the Vatican some 7 years,& then Czechoslovakia on the manor Líčkov. She became known as a democrat and a pacifist in her work and was a friend of president Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk. In 1938, 
2 CONC she returned to Sweden after an order for her arrest had been issued by the Gestapo. In 1940, she was one of the founders of the discussionclub Tisdagsklubben ("The Tuesday Club") in Stockholm. It was formally a discussion-club ab
2 CONC out culture, but its true purpose was to work against the expansion of nazism in Sweden. The club was in fact inaugurated the same day Nazi Germany occupied Norway, the 9 April 1940. Tisdagsklubben was to be used as the center of 
2 CONC the Swedish resistance movement in the case Sweden was ever occupied by Nazi Germany. Amelie Posse was, like other members of the club, listed in German records as "Untrustworthy Swedes".
2 CONT
2 CONT A tiny museum with Amelie Posse memorabilia can be found at Örenäs slott, close to Posse's childhood home (torn down), near Landskrona in south Sweden.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Amelie Posse, fd Posse-Brézdová, fd Bjerre, f. Posse. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Författare. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * https://www.skbl.se/en/article/AmeliePosse
2 CONT * Amelie Posse, http://www.skbl.se/sv/artikel/AmeliePosse, Svenskt kvinnobiografiskt lexikon (article by Eva Strömberg Krantz), retrieved 2023-04-14.
